==English==
{{wikipedia|Maquis}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|maquis}}, from {{der|en|co|machja}} (related to {{cog|it|macchia}}), ultimately from {{der|en|la|macula}}. {{doublet|en|macula}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmakiː/|/maˈkiː/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|botany}} Dense [[Mediterranean]] [[coastal]] [[scrub]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 27, 2007|author=Alida Becker|title=Season in the Sun|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/05/27/books/review/becker-t.html
|passage=The older man claims to find a measure of peace in Corsica’s wild landscape, and as Mitchell explores the foothills of '''maquis''', fragrant with “the sharp resinous smell of laurel rose and thyme,” he too succumbs.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} The French [[resistance]] movement during World War II, or other similar [[movement]]s elsewhere. {{defdate|from 1940s}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author=w:Alistair Horne|title=A Savage War of Peace|location=New York|publisher=Review Books|year_published=2006|page=75
|passage=By this time O.S. membership numbered some 4,500, and many of those who escaped imprisonment either fled abroad or formed the nucleus of a growing '''maquis''' in the more inaccessible parts of the country.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=03 Dec 83|volume=11|number=20|journal=Gay Community News|page=5|author=Gary Ralph|title=No Security for Those Who Never Had It|text=For many weeks after I finished ''Bodyguard of Lies'', Alan Turing stuck in my mind. He seemed so incongruous a figure to be mixed up with the cut-throat '''''maquis''''' and sinister double-agents who populated the rest of the book.}}

